{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":".opencode/entities/cli/CLI.RIPGREP.md"},"lines":{"text":"**Ripgrep** — a recursive regex search tool written in Rust by Andrew Gallant (BurntSushi), first released in 2016. It searches the current directory by default, respects `.gitignore` and `.ignore` rules, and writes matching lines to stdout with colorized output. The name combines \"rip\" — meaning fast, as in to rip through text — with \"grep\" to signal its speed.\n"},"line_number":1,"absolute_offset":0,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"regex"},"start":28,"end":33}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":".opencode/entities/cli/CLI.RIPGREP.md"},"lines":{"text":"Ripgrep uses the Rust `regex` crate with SIMD optimizations, which places it among the fastest line-oriented search tools. It supports PCRE2 via the `-P` flag, smart case detection with `-S`, and JSON output with `--json` for programmatic consumption. VS Code embeds it as its default search engine. It ignores binary files and respects gitignore semantics automatically.\n"},"line_number":3,"absolute_offset":372,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"regex"},"start":23,"end":28}]}}
